var a=[{title:`Pick a repeatable measure`,text:`Good options include how far you walk in a set time, how long a familiar route takes, whether you finish a set interval session, or your heart rate at a comfortable fixed pace. Session RPE adds to these numbers, especially if you don’t have a heart-rate monitor.`},{title:`Keep test conditions similar`,text:`Temperature, hills, sleep, caffeine, equipment and recovery all affect performance. When you can, compare the same treadmill setting, cycling resistance or outdoor route. A lower heart rate doesn’t always mean you’re fitter, because medication and measurement error can also change the reading.`},{title:`Example`,text:`A beginner starts by alternating two minutes of walking with one minute of gentle jogging. Weeks later, they finish more jogging intervals at a similar effort. Someone else rides the same cycling route and it feels easier, even though their average speed stays the same.`},{title:`How often to test`,text:`A planned check every few weeks shows trends while your normal sessions stay focused on training. A sudden drop in performance may come from fatigue or illness, and it doesn’t have to mean you’ve lost conditioning for good.`},{title:`Using TrainUp`,text:`After each workout you rate the effort from 1 to 10, and History shows your average effort and a 4-week chart. Look at the trend over weeks. A single heart-rate or speed number can’t diagnose your cardiovascular health or guarantee it.`}];var o=[{label:`NHS: walking for health`,url:`https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/exercise/walking-for-health/`},{label:`2026 ACSM position stand`,url:`https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41843416/`}];var r={sections:a,sources:o};export{r as default,a as sections,o as sources};